      "text": ":\nOkay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. So the question is about logical reasoning based on the given statements about George and composing. The task is to determine if the statement \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose\" is true, false, or uncertain. The options are A, B, or C. \n\nFirst, I need to parse the given context carefully. Let me restate the premises to make sure I understand them correctly:\n\n1. If George likes music, he wants to compose. (So, L \u2192 W)\n2. If George has access to a program, he can compose. (A \u2192 C)\n3. If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose. (C \u2227 W \u2192 K)\n\nThe question is about the truth value of the statement: \"If George will not compose, then George cannot compose.\" In logical terms, that would be \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC. \n\nWait a second, the original statement to evaluate is \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose.\" Let me make sure I translate that correctly. The antecedent is \"George will not compose\" (\u00acK), and the consequent is \"George cannot compose\" (\u00acC). So the statement is \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC. \n\nWe need to determine if this is necessarily true, necessarily false, or uncertain based on the given premises. \n\nLet me think about the contrapositive here. The contrapositive of \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC would be C \u2192 K. Because in logic, the contrapositive of \"If A then B\" is \"If not B then not A,\" and they are logically equivalent. So if the original statement is \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC, then its contrapositive is C \u2192 K. \n\nLooking at the third premise, which is C \u2227 W \u2192 K. That says that if both can compose and wants to compose, then he will compose. But the contrapositive of the statement we're evaluating is C \u2192 K. Wait, but the premise requires both C and W to get K. So if we have C alone, without knowing W, then we can't conclude K. \n\nTherefore, the contrapositive of our target