regardless. In this case, George will not compose (since K requires both C and W). So in this scenario, \u00acK is true, but C is true, so \u00acC is false. Therefore, the implication \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC would be true only if whenever \u00acK is true, \u00acC is also true. But in this case, \u00acK is true and \u00acC is false, making the implication false. Therefore, the statement \"If George will not compose, then he cannot compose\" is false because there exists a situation where he doesn't compose but can compose. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B) False. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to check if there's any other premises that might affect this. Let me check again. The first premise is about liking music leading to wanting to compose. The second is access to a program leading to can compose. The third is the combination of can and want leading to will compose. \n\nIn my example, if George can compose (so he has access to a program, from premise 2), but doesn't want to compose. Maybe he doesn't like music? Wait, if he doesn't want to compose, then according to the first premise, if he likes music (L), then he would want to (W). So if W is false, that would mean L must be false. Because L \u2192 W, so contrapositive is \u00acW \u2192 \u00acL. So in this scenario where W is false, L must be false. \n\nBut that's okay. The example still holds: George can compose (C is true) because he has access (A is true), but he doesn't want to compose (W is false because he doesn't like music). Thus, he doesn't compose (\u00acK). Therefore, in that case, the statement \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC is false because \u00acK is true and \u00acC is false. \n\nTherefore, the original statement is not always true; in fact, there's a case where it's false, so the answer is B) False. \n\nWait, but the question is asking whether the statement is true, false, or uncertain. Since there's a case where the statement is false, that makes the entire statement false, right? Because