ondition. \n\nSo, putting this together: To be in the Top 10, a game must be Japanese (necessary), and selling over a million is sufficient. So even if a Japanese game sold less than a million, it might still be in the Top 10 if there's another reason, but selling over a million guarantees it's there. \n\nTherefore, for LoZ, since it sold over a million, it must be in the Top 10, regardless of other factors. So the answer is A) True. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is A. The other options don't hold. The first premise about being Japanese is just an extra fact that's not needed here because the sales condition is already met. So the conclusion is certain.\n</think>\n\nThe problem provides the following premises:  \n1. The Legend of Zelda (LoZ) is made by a Japanese game company.  \n2. All games in the Top 10 list are made by Japanese companies (T \u2192 J).  \n3. If a game sells over one million copies, it will be in the Top 10 (S \u2192 T).  \n4. LoZ sold over one million copies (S(LoZ) = true).  \n\nFrom premise 3 (S \u2192 T) and premise 4 (S(LoZ)), we apply **modus ponens**: since LoZ meets the sales condition (S), it must be in the Top 10 (T). The first premise (J) is redundant here because the sales condition alone guarantees inclusion. The Top 10 list\u2019s requirement that all entries are Japanese (T \u2192 J) does not affect this conclusion because LoZ already satisfies the Japanese origin condition.  \n\nThus, the statement \"The Legend of Zelda is in the Top 10 list\" is **true**.  \n\n**Answer:** A) True",
