ises 2 and 1).  \n5. **If dried Thai chilies (DTC) are spicy **or** a bakery, then they are a hotpot **or** spicy.**  \n   \u2192 Symbolically: \\( (S \\lor B) \\rightarrow (H \\lor S) \\).  \n\n### Analysis:\n- **If DTC were a product of Baked by Melissa**, it would have to be a cupcake (Premise 4).  \n  - But cupcakes are bakeries (Premise 2), so DTC would be a bakery.  \n  - However, **Premise 5** shows this is impossible:  \n    - If DTC were a bakery (\\(B = \\text{true}\\)), then \\(S\\) (spicy) must be false (Premise 1).  \n    - The antecedent \\( (B \\lor S) \\) becomes true (since \\(B = \\text{true}\\)), requiring the consequent \\( (H \\lor S) \\) to be true.  \n    - Since \\(S = \\text{false}\\), \\(H\\) (hotpot) must be true.  \n    - But hotpots are spicy (Premise 3), so \\(H \\rightarrow S = \\text{true}\\).  \n    - This contradicts \\(S = \\text{false}\\) (from Premise 1).  \n    - Thus, **DTC cannot be a bakery** (or a product of Baked by Melissa).  \n\n- **Even if DTC is spicy**, it still cannot be a product of Baked by Melissa, as their products are exclusively non-spicy cupcakes.  \n\n### Conclusion:\nThe statement \"Dried Thai chilies are a product of Baked by Melissa\" is **false** because DTC cannot be a bakery (due to the logical contradiction in Premise 5) and all Baked by Melissa products are bakeries.  \n\n**Answer:** B) False",
