v(x): x is instructed by professor David.\n\n- Lab(x): x works in the lab.\n\nGiven statements:\n\n1. \u00ac\u2203x (L(x) \u2227 CS(x)) \u2192 All students in library are not in CS. So L(x) \u2192 \u00acCS(x).\n\n2. All P(x) are L(x): P(x) \u2192 L(x).\n\n3. All D(x) are CS(x): D(x) \u2192 CS(x).\n\n4. All Dav(x) are D(x): Dav(x) \u2192 D(x).\n\n5. All Lab(x) are Dav(x): Lab(x) \u2192 Dav(x).\n\nGiven that James is Lab(James).\n\nFrom Lab(James) \u2192 Dav(James) (by 5), then Dav(James) \u2192 D(James) (by 4), so D(James) \u2192 CS(James) (by 3). Thus, CS(James) is true.\n\nNow, since CS(James) is true, then from statement 1's contrapositive: CS(x) \u2192 \u00acL(x). Therefore, James does not work in the library.\n\nNow, if James had P(James), then by statement 2, P(James) \u2192 L(James). But we just saw L(James) is false. Therefore, P(James) must be false. Therefore, the statement is false. So answer is B.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires determining if James has a part-time job offered by the university based on the given premises. Here's the breakdown:\n\n1. **James works in the lab**:  \n   - By premise 5 (\"All students working in the lab are instructed by professor David\"), James is instructed by David.  \n   - From premise 4 (\"All students instructed by professor David are taking the database course\"), James takes the database course.  \n   - By premise 3 (\"All students who take the database course are from the computer science department\"), James is in the computer science (CS) department.  \n\n2. **Implications of being in CS**:  \n   - Premise 1 states \"No students who work in the library are from the CS department,\" which means **CS(x) \u2192 \u00acL(x)** (if someone is in CS, they do not work in the library). Since James is in CS, he does **not work in the library**.  \n\n3. **Part-time job requirement**:  \n   - Premise 2 states \"All students who have part-time jobs offered by the university work i