oblem states that Luke is in either one or the other. So if Case 1 leads to a contradiction, then Case 2 must be the case.\n\nLet me verify again.\n\nCase 1: T \u2227 B.\n\nThen:\n\n- T is true, so S must be true (from premise 1).\n\n- B is true, so L must be true (from premise 4's contrapositive B \u2192 L).\n\nBut then S \u2192 \u00acL (premise 5). Since S is true, \u00acL must be true. But \u00acL would mean Luke does not live at home regularly, but from B \u2192 L, L must be true. Contradiction. Therefore, this case is impossible. Therefore, Luke must be in Case 2.\n\nCase 2: \u00acT \u2227 \u00acB.\n\nSo, in this case, T is false, and B is false.\n\nWe need to see if this leads us to know S's value.\n\nFirst, T is false, so premise 1 (T \u2192 S) is trivially true (since antecedent is false), so that doesn't give us info about S.\n\nSince B is false, that's okay, but maybe other premises can help.\n\nWe need to see what we can say about S here. Let's see:\n\nPremise 5 is S \u2192 \u00acL. To find S's value, perhaps we need to see if we can find anything about L or other variables.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can see if there's any constraints on S from other premises.\n\nLet's see if there are other implications. Let's think of other premises involving S.\n\nPremise 1: T \u2192 S. Since T is false here, that doesn't force S to be anything.\n\nPremise 5: S \u2192 \u00acL. So if S were true, then \u00acL would have to be true (i.e., L is false). But if S is false, then the implication holds regardless.\n\nAdditionally, let's see other premises. For example, premise 2 and 3 involve SA and C. But we don't have info on whether Luke complains or has siblings the same age. Since in this case, T is false, from premise 2 (SA \u2192 T), if SA were true, then T would have to be true, but since T is false, SA must be false. So SA is false here.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, since T is false, SA must be false (from SA \u2192 T, contrapositive is \u00acT \u2192 \u00acSA). Theref