tatement says \"All severe cancers are deadly diseases,\" but not the converse. So just because something is a deadly disease doesn't necessarily mean it's a severe cancer. The converse isn't stated. \n\nTherefore, the only way to be a severe cancer is if it is explicitly in that category. The problem is, the only explicit severe cancer given is Bile Duct Cancer (since it's stated that \"Bile duct cancer is a severe cancer\"). So unless Colorectal Cancer is part of another category that's a severe cancer, which isn't mentioned here, we can't assume it. \n\nHowever, the last statement tells us that Colorectal Cancer is not a Bile Duct Cancer. So, if it were a severe cancer, it would have to be a severe cancer through another route, but there's no information given here about other severe cancers. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps if Colorectal Cancer is a deadly disease, then it would be a severe cancer? But the problem is, the second statement is \"All severe cancers are deadly diseases,\" which is a one-way implication. So being a deadly disease doesn't imply being a severe cancer. So unless Colorectal Cancer is a severe cancer via some other classification, but there's no information given here. \n\nWait, but the question is asking if the statement \"Colorectal cancer is a severe cancer\" is true, false, or uncertain. \n\nLet me recap the given information again to make sure I haven't missed anything.\n\nThe premises are:\n\n1. Deadly Disease \u2192 Low Survival Rate\n2. Severe Cancer \u2192 Deadly Disease\n3. Bile Duct Cancer \u2192 Severe Cancer\n4. Cholangiocarcinoma \u2192 Bile Duct Cancer (so Cholangiocarcinoma is a type of Bile Duct Cancer)\n5. Mild Flu \u2192 \u00acLow Survival Rate (contrapositive: Low Survival Rate \u2192 \u00acMild Flu)\n6. (Colorectal Cancer is Bile Duct Cancer \u2227 Survival Rate is Low) \u2192 \u00acColorectal Cancer is Bile Duct Cancer\n\nFrom premise 6, as analyzed earlier, this leads us to conclude that Colorectal Cancer is NOT a Bile Du