ds(x)) is true. \n\nGiven that there exists some x where Bird(x) and Hawk(x), and since all hawks don't land, those x's would have Bird(x) \u2227 \u00acLands(x). Therefore, the statement \u2200x (Bird(x) \u2192 Lands(x)) is false because there exists a counterexample. Therefore, the answer is B) False. \n\nI think that's solid. The key is that the existence of hawks (which are birds) that don't land directly contradicts the idea that all birds do land. So the answer is definitely B.\n</think>\n\nThe context states that \"A hawk never lands\" (meaning all hawks do not land) and \"Some birds are hawks\" (indicating hawks are a subset of birds). Since hawks are birds that never land, there exist birds (specifically hawks) that do not land. This directly contradicts the statement \"All birds land,\" making it **false**.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
