would be false. However, since the antecedent can't be true (because Tom can't be M), the implication is true. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is testing if we can see that the consequent's \u00acA is impossible, so the implication is true because the antecedent can't lead to the consequent. Wait, but the logic here is that the implication is only false if the antecedent is true and the consequent is false. Since the antecedent can't be true, the implication can't be false, so it's true.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be A) True.\n\nBut wait, another angle: The question might be trying to see if there's a case where the antecedent could be true. Let's suppose, hypothetically, that Tom were not grumpy and were mean to animals. Then, the consequent would require that Tom is neither grumpy nor an animal lover. But Tom is an animal lover, so the consequent would be false. Therefore, in this hypothetical scenario, the implication would be false. But since this scenario is impossible (because Tom can't be mean to animals), the implication as a whole is considered true in formal logic. So the implication is true because the impossible case can't make it false. Hence, the answer is A.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is phrased in a way that requires evaluating the statement without considering the premises? Wait no, the question says \"based on the above information,\" so we must use the premises.\n\nAnother check: Let me verify all premises again.\n\nPremise 1: No one nice to animals is mean to animals. So N \u2192 \u00acM.\n\nPremise 2: Some grumpy people are mean to animals. So there exists some people in G \u2229 M.\n\nPremise 3: All animal lovers are nice to animals. A \u2192 N.\n\nPremise 4: If someone owns a pet, then they love animals. P \u2192 A.\n\nPremise 5: Tom is a pet owner (P is true for Tom).\n\nTherefore, Tom has P \u2192 A, so Tom is A. Then A \u2192 N, so Tom is N. Then N \u2192 \u00acM, so Tom is \u00acM. So Tom cannot be M. Therefore, in any