sidered true. Therefore, the correct answer is A.\n\nHmm, I think I've confirmed this multiple times. So the answer should be A.\n</think>\n\nThe given context establishes that Tom, as a pet owner, is an animal lover (A), which makes him nice to animals (N). Since no one nice to animals is mean to them (\u00acM), Tom cannot be mean to animals (M is false). The statement to evaluate is: *If Tom is not grumpy (\u00acG) and mean to animals (M), then he is neither grumpy (\u00acG) nor an animal lover (\u00acA)*. \n\n- **Antecedent**: \u00acG \u2227 M \u2192 False (since M is false).  \n- **Consequent**: \u00acG \u2227 \u00acA \u2192 False (since A is true).  \n\nIn logic, an implication with a false antecedent is always true, regardless of the consequent. Thus, the statement is **True** because the antecedent\u2019s impossibility ensures the implication holds.  \n\n**Answer: A) True**",
