ol dances.\" So, I \u2192 C, where I is \"inactive and disinterested member.\"\n\nFifth statement: \"All young children and teenagers who wish to further their academic careers and educational opportunities are students who attend the school.\" Let me parse this. Let Y be \"young child or teenager who wishes to further academic career...\" So Y \u2192 S.\n\nSixth statement about Bonnie: \"Bonnie either both attends and is very engaged with school events and is a student who attends the school, or she neither attends and is very engaged with school events nor is a student who attends the school.\" So, in symbols, (A \u2227 S) \u2228 (\u00acA \u2227 \u00acS). So Bonnie's status is such that she is in one of those two states: either she both attends and is engaged (A) and is a student (S), or she is neither (\u00acA and \u00acS). This is like a biconditional between A and S for Bonnie? Wait, actually, that's exactly the case. Because (A \u2227 S) \u2228 (\u00acA \u2227 \u00acS) is equivalent to (A \u2194 S). So Bonnie's A and S are equivalent. So if she's a student, then she is engaged, and vice versa, or if she's not a student, she's not engaged, etc.\n\nNow the question is about evaluating the truth of this statement:\n\n\"If Bonnie is either both a young child or teenager who wishes to further her academic career and educational opportunities and chaperones high school dances or neither is a young child nor teenager who wishes to further her academic career and educational opportunities, then Bonnie is either a student who attends the school or is an inactive and disinterested member of the community.\"\n\nBreaking this down. Let me denote:\n\nLet Y = Bonnie is a young child or teenager who wishes to further her academic career and educational opportunities.\n\nLet C = Bonnie chaperones high school dances.\n\nThe antecedent (the \"if\" part) is: (Y \u2227 C) \u2228 (\u00acY \u2227 \u00acC). Wait, actually, the wording says \"either both Y and C or neither Y nor C\".