pill a lot of food on their clothing. So, F \u2192 S, where F is \"foodie who goes out frequently\", S is \"spills a lot\".\n\n3. **Third statement**: If people grew up as only children (O), then they tidy consistently and enjoy cleaning. So O \u2192 (Tidy \u2227 Enjoy). \n\n4. **Fourth statement**: If people have parents very busy with jobs (B), then they grew up as only children. B \u2192 O.\n\n5. **Fifth statement**: All households with high net income (H) have people who have parents very busy. So H \u2192 B.\n\n6. **Peter's condition**: Peter is either (S \u2227 O) or (\u00acS \u2227 \u00acO). So, Peter is in the case where he either spills and is an only child, or neither spills nor is an only child. That's an exclusive OR? Wait, the wording is \"either ... or ...\" which can sometimes be inclusive, but the way it's phrased here is \"either X or Y\" where Y is the negation of both parts of X. Wait, actually, the exact wording is: \"Peter is either both a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and grew up as an only child with no siblings, or he is neither a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing nor grew up as an only child with no siblings.\" So that's an exclusive OR between (S \u2227 O) and (\u00acS \u2227 \u00acO). Because those are the two possibilities. So Peter can't be in any other combination. So his options are either (S and O) or (not S and not O). So those are the only two possibilities for Peter.\n\nNow, the question is: Is the statement \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try (F) or belongs to a household with a high net income (H)\" true, false, or uncertain?\n\nLet me break down each part.\n\nFirst, let's consider Peter's possible scenarios:\n\nCase 1: Peter is (S \u2227 O)\n\nCase 2: Peter is (\u00acS \u2227 \u00acO)\n\nWe need to see in each case whether the conclusion holds (F \u2228 H).\n\nStarting with Case 1: S \u2227 O.\n\nIf Peter is S (spills a lot), then from the first statement, since he spills, he must \u00acTidy and \u00acEnjoy. But from the third statement, since he is O (only child), O \u2192 (Tidy \u2227 Enjoy). Wait, this is a contradiction. Because if he is O, then he must tidy and enjoy, but if he spills, he can't. Therefore, this scenario (S \u2227 O) leads to a contradiction. Therefore, this case cannot happen. Therefore, Peter cannot be in Case 1.\n\nWait, that's a key point. Let me verify again.\n\nIf Peter is in Case 1 (S \u2227 O), then:\n\nFrom S (spills), we have from first statement: \u00acTidy and \u00acEnjoy.\n\nFrom O (only child), third statement says O \u2192 Tidy \u2227 Enjoy. So if O is true, then Tidy and Enjoy must both be true. But S leads to \u00acTidy and \u00acEnjoy. Therefore, Tidy is both true and false? That's impossible. Therefore, the scenario (S \u2227 O) is impossible. So Peter cannot be in that case. Therefore, the only possible case Peter can be in is Case 2: \u00acS \u2227 \u00acO.\n\nTherefore, Peter must be \u00acS (does not spill) and \u00acO (not an only child).\n\nNow, let's analyze Case 2: \u00acS \u2227 \u00acO.\n\nWe need to see whether in this case, Peter must be either F (foodie) or H (high income household).\n\nFirst, let's see if F implies anything here. Since F \u2192 S (from second statement). But Peter is \u00acS, so F would require S to be true, which it isn't. Therefore, Peter cannot be F (because if he were, he would have to spill, which he doesn't). Therefore, F is false for Peter.\n\nNow, the other part is H (high income household). Let's see if H must be true here.\n\nH implies B (parents busy) via the fifth statement: H \u2192 B. But the contrapositive is \u00acB \u2192 \u00acH. But we need to see if Peter must have H or not. Wait, but to prove that Peter must be H or F, but since F is out, we need to see if H must be true.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Peter could be in \u00acH as well. Let's see.\n\nSince Peter is \u00acO (not an only child), from the fourth statement: B \u