th a contradiction in the consequent would only be true if the antecedent is false. So (W \u2227 M) \u2192 (\u00acP \u2227 \u00acM) is equivalent to \u00ac(W \u2227 M) \u2228 (\u00acP \u2227 \u00acM). But since (\u00acP \u2227 \u00acM) can be true even if W and M are not both true, but if W and M are both true, then the consequent must hold, which would require \u00acM, so that's a contradiction. Therefore, this implication is essentially saying that (W \u2227 M) cannot happen. So, Mary cannot both work and need money. Wait, but that contradicts the first premise. The first premise says that if someone works (W), then they need money (M). So W \u2192 M. Therefore, if Mary works (W), then she must need money (M). But the sixth premise says that if Mary both works and needs money, then she doesn't need money. Which is a contradiction. Therefore, this must mean that the antecedent of the sixth premise can't be true. Therefore, Mary cannot have both W and M. But according to the first premise, if she has W, then she must have M. Therefore, the only way that can happen is if Mary does not work (\u00acW). Because if she did work, then M must be true, but then the sixth premise would require \u00acM, leading to a contradiction, so that can't be. Therefore, Mary must not work? Wait, but the problem is asking about Mary's situation where she does work and needs money. Wait, the question is about a statement involving Mary, but the premises include a conditional about Mary. Let me re-examine the problem's question.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\" So this is a general conditional statement, not necessarily stating that Mary does or does not work. Wait, actually, the premises are all general statements except for the last one, which is specifically about Mary? Or are all premises general excep