e company or not. Therefore the answer should be C) Uncertain.\n\nWait, but wait. Let me think again. The problem says \"James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\" So it's an exclusive OR? Or inclusive? The wording is \"either... or...\" which usually is exclusive, but sometimes in logic it's inclusive unless stated otherwise. Wait, the exact wording is \"James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\" So the two possibilities are:\n\nEither (Manager \u2227 Appears) or (\u00acManager \u2227 \u00acAppears). These are the only two possibilities. So he can't be, say, a manager and not appear, or not a manager but appear. Those options are excluded. \n\nTherefore, the two cases are the only possibilities. Since in one case he must have lunch in the company, and in the other he must have lunch at home, and we don't know which case he is in, the answer is uncertain. So the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nC) Uncertain\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Case 1 (James is a manager and appears in the company):**\n   - As a manager, he cannot work remotely (per \"No managers work remotely\").\n   - If he had lunch at home, he would work remotely, which is impossible. Thus, he must have lunch at the company.\n   - **Conclusion:** In this case, the statement \"James has lunch in the company\" is **True**.\n\n2. **Case 2 (James is not a manager and does not appear in the company):**\n   - Since he doesn\u2019t appear, he couldn\u2019t have scheduled a meeting (as scheduling a meeting requires appearing).\n   - If he had lunch at the company, he would have had to schedule a meeting (per \"Everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings\"), but he didn\u2019t. Thus, he must have lunch at home.\n   - **Conclusion:** In this case, the statement \"James has lunch in the company\" is **False**.\n\n3. **Uncertainty:**\n   - The problem specifies James must be in one of the two cases but provides no further details to determine which case applies.\n   - Both scenarios are logically consistent with the premises, so the answer depends on unknown information.\n\n**Final Answer:** Uncertain (C)",
