nt \"Some humans are horses\" is false. \n\nWait, but the options are true, false, uncertain. So the answer should be B) False. \n\nI don't see any uncertainty here because the premises are stated as absolute. There's no possibility left open for some overlap. So the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe context states that all horses have hooves, and no humans have hooves. To determine if \"Some humans are horses\" is true, false, or uncertain, we analyze the logical implications:\n\n1. **Premise 1**: All horses have hooves.  \n   - This means if something is a horse, it must have hooves.  \n2. **Premise 2**: No humans have hooves.  \n   - This means humans are entirely excluded from the set of beings with hooves.  \n\nIf \"some humans are horses\" were true, those humans would need to satisfy both being horses (and thus having hooves) and being humans (who cannot have hooves). This creates a contradiction, as the premises explicitly rule out the possibility of overlap between humans and horses.  \n\nUsing syllogistic logic:  \n- From \"All horses have hooves\" \u2192 \"No non-hoofed beings are horses.\"  \n- Since \"No humans have hooves,\" humans fall into the category of non-hoofed beings.  \n- Therefore, **no humans can be horses**.  \n\nThe conclusion is definitive based on the given premises.  \n\n**Answer**: B) False",
