      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to figure out if \"Black Mirror\" is popular based on the given information. Let me go through each statement one by one and see what I can deduce.\n\nFirst, the context says that \"Stranger Things\" is a popular Netflix show. Then, there's a conditional: if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. So, popularity implies binge-watching by Karen. \n\nNext, it says that Karen will download a Netflix show if and only if she binge-watches it. That's an \"if and only if\" statement, which means both directions. So, she downloads it only if she binge-watched, and if she binge-watched, she must have downloaded it. So, downloading and binge-watching are equivalent here.\n\nThen, we're told that Karen does not download \"Black Mirror\". Since \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show, that's given too. \n\nThe last statement is that if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it with Lisa. \n\nThe question is whether \"Black Mirror\" is popular. The options are True, False, Uncertain.\n\nLet me structure the statements symbolically to make it clearer.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet P(X) = \"X is popular\"\n\nB(X) = \"Karen binge-watches X\"\n\nD(X) = \"Karen downloads X\"\n\nS(X) = \"Karen shares X with Lisa\"\n\nGiven:\n\n1. P(\"Stranger Things\") \u2013 given directly.\n\n2. For any Netflix show X, if P(X) then B(X). So P(X) \u2192 B(X).\n\n3. For any Netflix show X, B(X) \u2194 D(X). So B(X) if and only if D(X).\n\n4. \u00acD(\"Black Mirror\") \u2013 Karen does not download Black Mirror.\n\n5. \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show.\n\n6. For any Netflix show X, if B(X) then S(X). So B(X) \u2192 S(X).\n\nWe need to determine if P(\"Black Mirror\") is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nStarting with Black Mirror (let's abbreviate as BM). We know BM is a Netflix show. From statement 4, \u00acD(BM). Since B \u2194 D, then \u00acD(BM) implies \u00acB(BM). Because if D were true, then B would have to be true, but since D is false, B m