edding (as in both cases above), then he can't have a Paris honeymoon. \n\nSo, in either case, John cannot have a wedding, so he cannot have a wedding in July, or a well-attended one, etc.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"John has a wedding that is well-attended\" must be false, because he can't have a wedding at all. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut let me check again the sixth premise. The sixth premise says that if John has a well-attended wedding, then either his wedding is in July or he goes to Paris. But since he can't have a wedding at all, this is a vacuous truth. It doesn't affect the conclusion.\n\nAnother angle: Suppose we try to assume that John does have a well-attended wedding. Let's see if that leads to a contradiction.\n\nAssume WellAttendedWedding (WAW). Then, by premise 6, either his wedding is in July (JulyWedding) or he travels to Paris (ParisHoneymoon). \n\nCase 1: JulyWedding is true. Then, by premise 3, JulyWedding \u2192 ParisHoneymoon. So, he must have ParisHoneymoon. \n\nSo, he has ParisHoneymoon. But from premise 2, ParisHoneymoon \u2192 Wedding. So he must have a wedding. \n\nSo, he has a wedding. Then, by premise 1, he must have a child (HasChild). \n\nBut then, by premise 5: HasChild \u2192 \u00acParisHoneymoon \u2227 \u00acWedding. But we just established that he does have ParisHoneymoon and Wedding, which contradicts the conclusion of premise 5. \n\nTherefore, this case leads to a contradiction.\n\nCase 2: Instead of JulyWedding, he travels to Paris (ParisHoneymoon). Then, again, by premise 2, ParisHoneymoon \u2192 Wedding. So he has a wedding. Then again, by premise 1, he has a child. Then by premise 5, he cannot have a wedding or ParisHoneymoon. Contradiction again.\n\nTherefore, assuming that John has a well-attended wedding leads to a contradiction. Therefore, the statement must be false. So the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nB) False\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Premise 1:** All people with a wedding have at l