      "text": ":\nOkay, let's try to tackle this problem step by step. First, I need to understand all the given statements and see how they relate to Taylor's situation. The question is asking whether the statement \"Taylor neither grew up in a health-conscious childhood home nor does she visit the gym at least once a day\" is true, false, or uncertain based on the context provided. The options are A, B, or C. \n\nLet me start by breaking down each of the premises given in the context. I'll list them out one by one and see how they connect.\n\n1. All people who eat salads regularly are very conscious about their health and eating habits. \n   - So, Salad Eaters \u2192 Health Conscious (HC). \n\n2. All people who grew up in health-conscious childhood homes (let's call this G) eat salads regularly.\n   - G \u2192 Salad Eaters.\n\n3. All people who fulfill their nutritional daily intakes (F) grew up in health-conscious childhood homes (G).\n   - F \u2192 G.\n\n4. If people have good relationships with their parents (R), then they fulfill their nutritional daily intakes (F).\n   - R \u2192 F.\n\n5. If people have good relationships with their parents (R), then they do not eat salads regularly (\u00acSalad).\n   - R \u2192 \u00acSalad.\n\n6. If people visit the gym at least once a day (Gym), then they always fulfill their nutritional daily intakes (F).\n   - Gym \u2192 F.\n\n7. It is either both true that Taylor grew up in a health-conscious childhood home (G) and she has a good relationship with her parents (R), or it is true that Taylor neither grew up in a health-conscious childhood home nor has a good relationship with her parents (\u00acG \u2227 \u00acR). \n   - This is an exclusive OR (XOR) between (G \u2227 R) and (\u00acG \u2227 \u00acR). So, (G \u2227 R) XOR (\u00acG \u2227 \u00acR). \n\nThe question is about whether \"Taylor neither grew up in a health-conscious childhood home nor does she visit the gym at least once a day\" is true, false, or uncertain. In logical terms, this is equivalent to saying \u00acG \u2227 \u00acGym. We need to determine if this must be true, must be false, or is unknown based on the premises.\n\nFirst, let me note that the seventh premise gives us two possibilities for Taylor's situation regarding G and R. Either she has both G and R, or she has neither G nor R. So, these are the only two options for G and R for Taylor.\n\nLet me analyze both cases separately. \n\nCase 1: Taylor has both G (grew up health-conscious) and R (good relationship with parents). \n\nCase 2: Taylor has neither G nor R (\u00acG \u2227 \u00acR).\n\nWe need to see what each case implies about Gym and other variables, and then see if in either case the statement \u00acG \u2227 \u00acGym is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nStarting with Case 1: G and R are both true.\n\nFrom premise 2: G \u2192 Salad Eaters. Since G is true, Taylor eats salads regularly.\n\nFrom premise 5: R \u2192 \u00acSalad. But here, since R is true, this would mean \u00acSalad must be true. However, in this case, Salad is true (from G\u2192Salad). This is a contradiction. Wait a second, that can't be. Because if R is true, then from premise 5, she can't eat salads, but from G being true, she must eat salads. Therefore, this case (G \u2227 R) leads to a contradiction. Therefore, this case is impossible. \n\nWait, that's a problem. Let me check again. \n\nIf Taylor has both G and R, then:\n\n- G \u2192 Salad (so she eats salads).\n\n- R \u2192 \u00acSalad (so she does not eat salads). \n\nThat's a direct contradiction. Therefore, the case where G and R are both true cannot exist. So the first part of premise 7's XOR is impossible. Therefore, the only possible case is the second part: Taylor neither G nor R (\u00acG \u2227 \u00acR). \n\nBecause the exclusive OR between (G \u2227 R) and (\u00acG \u2227 \u00acR) requires that exactly one of them is true. Since (G \u2227 R) leads to a contradiction, it must be false, so the other must be true. Therefore, Taylor