illers, which Peter is not. So, no constraints there.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that we can't determine whether Peter tidies and enjoys, so the answer is uncertain. So option C.\n\nWait, but let me check again to make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me go through each premise again:\n\n1. Spill \u2192 \u00acTidy \u2227 \u00acEnjoy (assuming that's the correct interpretation)\n2. Foodie \u2227 FrequentEater \u2192 Spill\n3. OnlyChild \u2192 Tidy \u2227 Enjoy\n4. BusyParents \u2192 OnlyChild\n5. HighIncome \u2192 BusyParents\n6. Peter is (Spill \u2227 OnlyChild) or (\u00acSpill \u2227 \u00acOnlyChild)\n\nWe determined that (Spill \u2227 OnlyChild) leads to contradiction (from 1 and 3), so Peter must be \u00acSpill \u2227 \u00acOnlyChild.\n\nNow, with Peter being \u00acOnlyChild and \u00acSpill, can we derive anything about his tidying?\n\nLooking at premise 3, since he's not an only child, that doesn't tell us anything. The contrapositive of 3 is if \u00ac(Tidy \u2227 Enjoy), then \u00acOnlyChild. But since he's already \u00acOnlyChild, that doesn't help. So, Tidy and Enjoy could be anything.\n\nThe other premises don't connect to tidying except via spill, which he doesn't do. So, yes, his tidying status is unknown. Therefore, the answer is C) Uncertain.\n\nWait, but maybe there's a different interpretation of the first premise? Let me check again. The first sentence says:\n\n\"If people spill a lot of food on their clothing, then they do not tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning.\"\n\nThe phrasing here could be ambiguous. Maybe it's parsed as \"do not [tidy consistently and enjoy cleaning]\" (i.e., both not tidy and not enjoy), which is how I took it. But another possible interpretation is \"do not tidy their spaces consistently, and [they] enjoy the process of cleaning.\" That would be Spill \u2192 (\u00acTidy \u2227 Enjoy). But that would make the first premise say spillers don't tidy but do enjoy. That would change things.\n\nHmm,