s not the usual reading. The standard parsing would be the first. So I think the analysis is correct.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires evaluating the truth of the statement: \"If Yuri is not an American basketball player, then Yuri is a basketball player,\" based on the given premises.\n\n**Key Steps:**\n1. **Premise Analysis:**\n   - All basketball players are tall (Premise 2).\n   - Tallness implies wearing large shoes (Premise 3), which in turn implies large-sized clothes (Premise 4).\n   - Yuri does not wear large-sized clothes (Premise 5).\n\n2. **Deductions about Yuri:**\n   - Since Yuri doesn\u2019t wear large clothes (\u00acLc(Yuri)), Yuri cannot wear large shoes (\u00acLs(Yuri)) (via contrapositive of Premise 4).\n   - \u00acLs(Yuri) implies Yuri isn\u2019t tall (\u00acT(Yuri)) (contrapositive of Premise 3).\n   - \u00acT(Yuri) means Yuri isn\u2019t a basketball player (\u00acB(Yuri)) (contrapositive of Premise 2).\n\n3. **Evaluating the Statement:**\n   - The antecedent (\"Yuri is not an American basketball player\") translates to \u00ac(B(Yuri) \u2227 A(Yuri)). Since \u00acB(Yuri) is true (Yuri isn\u2019t a basketball player), this antecedent is **true** (because B(Yuri) \u2227 A(Yuri) is false).\n   - The consequent (\"Yuri is a basketball player\") is **false** (since B(Yuri) is false).\n   - An implication (if true, then false) is **false**.\n\n**Conclusion:** The statement is false because the antecedent is true while the consequent is false. \n\n**Answer:** B) False",
