ed.\n\nWe need to consider two cases for James: either he is a manager and appears, or he's neither a manager nor appears.\n\nAdditionally, the antecedent of the statement we're evaluating is (M \u2228 C). So if either M or C is true, then we have to see whether the consequent holds.\n\nWe need to check for all possible scenarios where (M \u2228 C) is true, and see if the consequent must hold, or not.\n\nLet me first consider possible scenarios where (M \u2228 C) is true.\n\nCase 1: James is a manager (M is true). Since M is true, the antecedent (M\u2228C) is true, so we need to check the consequent.\n\nCase 2: James is in other countries (C is true). Then antecedent holds, check consequent.\n\nBut also, since the premises might link these variables, we need to see how they interact.\n\nLet me first handle Case 1: James is a manager (M = true).\n\nFrom premise 6: No managers work remotely. So M \u2192 \u00acR. Since James is a manager, then R must be false (\u00acR).\n\nSo R is false. Therefore, in the consequent, (H \u2227 R) would be false, so the consequent becomes [False \u2228 (\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR)]. Since \u00acR is true (because R is false), then (\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR) is equivalent to (\u00acH \u2227 True) \u2192 \u00acH. So the consequent is (False \u2228 \u00acH) \u2192 \u00acH. Wait, no, let me rephrase:\n\nWait, the consequent is (H \u2227 R) \u2228 (\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR). Since R is false, then (H \u2227 R) is false. The second part is (\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR). Since R is false, \u00acR is true, so that becomes (\u00acH \u2227 True) \u2192 \u00acH. So the entire consequent is False \u2228 \u00acH \u2192 \u00acH. Therefore, the consequent is equivalent to \u00acH. So the implication for this case would be:\n\nIf (M \u2228 C) then [\u00acH], given that M is true (so antecedent is true). Wait, but actually, let's track again.\n\nWait, let me re-express:\n\nGiven M is true (so antecedent holds), then the consequent is (H \u2227 R) \u2228 (\u00acH \u2