out Peter: \"Peter is either both a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and grew up as an only child with no siblings, or he is neither a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing nor grew up as an only child with no siblings.\" In logical terms, that's (S \u2227 OnlyChild) \u2228 (\u00acS \u2227 \u00acOnlyChild). So Peter is in one of those two exclusive cases.\n\nNow the question is: Is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and belong to a household with a high net income or he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try nor belongs to a household with a high net income.\"\n\nWait, the wording here is a bit confusing. Let me parse it again. The statement says:\n\n\"Peter is either [a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try (FGF) AND belongs to a household with a high net income (HighIncome)] OR [he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try (FGF) NOR belongs to a household with a high net income].\"\n\nWait, the second part says \"nor belongs to a household with a high net income.\" The use of \"nor\" here might be tricky. The structure is \"either (A and B) or (A nor B)\". Alternatively, maybe it's written as \"either (FGF and HighIncome) or (FGF nor HighIncome)\", but \"nor\" typically requires both parts to be negated. Alternatively, maybe the question is phrased as \"either (FGF and HighIncome) or (FGF and not HighIncome)\", but the wording uses \"nor\", which might mean \"neither FGF nor HighIncome\". Hmm, perhaps there's a misinterpretation here. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal question statement: \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and belong to a household with a high net income or he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try nor belongs to a household with a high net income.\"\