hich he isn't. Therefore, Peter cannot be in a HighIncome household. So HighIncome must be false for Peter.\n\nTherefore, Peter is \u00acFGF and \u00acHighIncome. \n\nSo the statement in question is: (FGF \u2227 HI) \u2228 (\u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHI). Since Peter is \u00acFGF and \u00acHI, the second part of the OR is true (because \u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHI is true). Therefore, the entire statement is true. Wait, but the options are A) True, B) False, C) Uncertain. So according to this reasoning, the answer would be A) True?\n\nWait, but let me check again. The statement says \"either (FGF and HI) or (\u00acFGF nor HI)\". Wait, maybe my initial interpretation was wrong. Let me re-examine the original question's wording again.\n\nThe question states: \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and belong to a household with a high net income or he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try nor belongs to a household with a high net income.\"\n\nThe second part says \"he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try nor belongs to a household with a high net income.\" The wording here is a bit ambiguous. The phrase \"nor belongs\" could be parsed as \"nor [does he] belong\", which would make the second part \"\u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHighIncome\". Alternatively, it could be read as \"he is a foodie who goes out... nor belongs...\", which might mean that he is neither a foodie nor belongs. But given the structure, \"either (A and B) or (C nor D)\" is a bit unclear. Alternatively, perhaps it's a miswritten XOR, but given the options, let's see:\n\nIf the second part is \"nor belongs\" meaning \"does not belong\", then the second part is FGF and \u00acHighIncome. Wait, but \"nor\" typically requires two negations. For example, \"neither FGF nor belongs\" would be \u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHighIncome. But the wording is \"a foodie who goes out... nor belongs\", which might mean \"