d with a high net income.\"\n\nWait, the second part says \"he is a foodie who goes out... nor belongs...\". The structure here might actually be that the second part is \"he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try nor belongs...\", where \"nor\" applies to both being a foodie and belonging. That would make the second part \"\u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHighIncome\". So the entire statement is (FGF \u2227 HI) \u2228 (\u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHI), which is exactly what I thought before. Since Peter is \u00acFGF and \u00acHI, then the second part is true, so the entire statement is true. So A is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is phrased as \"either (FGF and HI) or (FGF nor HI)\", but \"nor\" would mean \"\u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHI\", making the second part (\u00acFGF \u2227 \u00acHI). So same as before.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires analyzing the given logical statements to determine the validity of a specific claim about Peter. Here's the breakdown:\n\n### Key Logical Statements:\n1. **Spilling Food (S) \u2192 \u00ac(Tidy \u2227 Enjoy):** If someone spills food, they neither tidy consistently nor enjoy cleaning.\n2. **Foodie Going Frequently (FGF) \u2192 S:** All foodies who go out frequently spill food.\n3. **Only Child (OC) \u2192 (Tidy \u2227 Enjoy):** Only children tidy and enjoy cleaning.\n4. **Busy Parents (BP) \u2192 OC:** Busy parents imply being an only child.\n5. **High Net Income (HI) \u2192 BP:** High-income households have busy parents.\n6. **Peter\u2019s Condition:** Peter is either (S \u2227 OC) or (\u00acS \u2227 \u00acOC).\n\n### Analysis of Peter\u2019s Case:\n- **Case 1 (S \u2227 OC):** Contradiction arises because:\n  - From **S**, Peter cannot **Tidy \u2227 Enjoy** (violates statement 1).\n  - From **OC**, Peter must **Tidy \u2227 Enjoy** (statement 3).\n  - **Impossible**, so Peter must be in **Case 2 (\u00acS \u2227 \u00acOC)**.\n\n### Implications of Case 2 